<p>A return is a departure from the UK by someone with no right to remain. The Home Office
publishes several counts of them, on different bases, and they are among the easiest figures
on this subject to add up wrongly. Two things decide almost every misreading.</p>

<p><strong>They count events, not people.</strong> One person returned twice appears twice.
No published figure here is a headcount of individuals, so
"{% figure "asylum/returns-enforced-plus-voluntary" %} people were returned" is not a claim
any of these numbers supports, however often the total is written that way.</p>

<p><strong>The categories do not nest.</strong> One of them is published separately from the
headline total on purpose, and one is a subset of it. Neither can be added to it.</p>

<h2>What the Home Office publishes</h2>

