<p>Only the first two rows sum to the third. Someone who adds the port refusals to the
headline total produces a larger number that the Home Office does not publish and that
counts two different things at once. Someone who adds the asylum-related figure counts part
of the total twice.</p>

<h2>Why port refusals are not returns in the usual sense</h2>

<p>A person refused entry at the border has not been living in the UK and is not being
removed from it. They are being turned away at the point of arrival. The Home Office counts
this separately for that reason, and this site holds the figure specifically so that the
separation is visible rather than assumed. It is
{% figure "asylum/returns-refused-entry-at-port" %} for the year ending June 2026, and it
belongs beside the returns total rather than inside it.</p>

<h2>Only some returns are asylum-related</h2>

<p>Of the headline total, {% figure "asylum/returns-asylum-related" %} were asylum-related:
people whose asylum claim had been refused, withdrawn or otherwise concluded. The rest
involve people with no asylum claim at all, such as visa overstayers and people
refused permission to stay after entering. <strong>A returns total is not an asylum statistic</strong>, and one quoted in an
argument about asylum is describing a larger population than the argument is about.</p>

<h2>What these figures cannot tell you</h2>

<p>They do not say how many individuals left, because they count events. <strong>And they do
not say how many people who should have left did not</strong>, because the denominator that
question needs is not published. The Home Office does not estimate it and says so in terms:
&ldquo;It is not possible to know the exact number of people currently resident in the UK
without permission, nor the total number of people who enter the UK irregularly, and so we do
not seek to make any official estimates of the illegal population.&rdquo; Independent
researchers have produced estimates, which the
<a href="https://migrationobservatory.ox.ac.uk/resources/briefings/unauthorised-migration-in-the-uk/">Migration
Observatory collects</a> and warns are &ldquo;highly uncertain and have large margins of
error&rdquo;. <strong>The most widely quoted of them was retracted in March 2025 and revised
downwards</strong>, which is worth knowing if you have met it in an argument.</p>

<p>Nor do the returns figures measure enforcement effort against any total: there is no
published denominator to divide them by. A return rate, in the sense of returns as a
share of people liable to be returned, is not a figure that exists.</p>

<p>Every figure on this page is flagged <strong>provisional</strong> by the Home Office, which
means it can be revised in later releases. See
<a href="/what-the-words-mean/#statistical-status">provisional, revised and official
statistics in development</a>, and <a href="/what-the-words-mean/#return">return</a> for the
definitions in full.</p>

